## What Is JSX?

**JSX** stands for **JavaScript XML**. It's a syntax extension that lets you write HTML-like code directly inside JavaScript.

```jsx
// JSX looks like HTML but lives inside JavaScript
const element = <h1>Hello, World!</h1>
```

Babel compiles JSX to `React.createElement()` calls:

```jsx
// What you write vs what Babel compiles
// You write:
const element = <h1 className="title">Hello</h1>

// Babel compiles to:
const element = React.createElement('h1', { className: 'title' }, 'Hello')
```

---

## Rule 1: Return a Single Root Element

```jsx
// ❌ WRONG — Two sibling elements without a wrapper
function App() {
  return (
    <h1>Hello</h1>
    <p>World</p>
  )
}

// ✅ CORRECT — Use a div wrapper
function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      <p>World</p>
    </div>
  )
}

// ✅ ALSO CORRECT — Use a Fragment (no extra DOM node!)
function App() {
  return (
    <>
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      <p>World</p>
    </>
  )
}
```

---

## Rule 2: Close All Tags

```jsx
// ❌ Wrong — unclosed tags
<img src="photo.jpg">
<input type="text">

// ✅ Correct — self-closing tags
<img src="photo.jpg" />
<input type="text" />
<br />
```

---

## Rule 3: className Instead of class

```jsx
// ❌ Wrong — class is a reserved JS keyword
<div class="container">Hello</div>

// ✅ Correct
<div className="container">Hello</div>
```

---

## Rule 4: camelCase Attributes

```jsx
// HTML vs JSX attribute names
// onclick     →  onClick
// onchange    →  onChange
// tabindex    →  tabIndex
// for         →  htmlFor

<label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
<input id="email" tabIndex={1} />
```

---

## Embedding JavaScript in JSX

```jsx
// Use curly braces {} to embed any JS expression
function Greeting() {
  const name = "Asif"
  const age = 25
  const isLoggedIn = true

  return (
    <div>
      {/* JSX Comment */}
      <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>
      <p>You will be {age + 1} next year.</p>
      <p>{isLoggedIn ? "Welcome back!" : "Please log in."}</p>
      <p>Year: {new Date().getFullYear()}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Inline Styles in JSX

```jsx
// Inline styles use an object with camelCase CSS properties
function StyledBox() {
  const boxStyle = {
    backgroundColor: '#4f46e5',
    color: 'white',
    padding: '20px',
    borderRadius: '8px',
    fontSize: '18px',
  }

  return <div style={boxStyle}>I am a styled box!</div>
}
```

---

## Chapter Summary

- JSX is HTML-like syntax compiled by Babel to JavaScript
- Always return one root element — use `<>` fragments to avoid extra DOM nodes
- Use `className`, `htmlFor`, and camelCase for all attributes
- Embed JavaScript in JSX using curly braces `{}` — expressions only

## What Is a Component?

A component is a **reusable, self-contained piece of UI**. Think of it as a custom HTML element you define yourself.

Components let you write UI once and reuse it anywhere, making code easy to maintain and test.

---

## Creating a Functional Component

A functional component is a JavaScript function that:
1. Starts with a **capital letter** (required!)
2. **Returns JSX**

```jsx
// A simple functional component
function WelcomeMessage() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Welcome to React!</h2>
      <p>You are learning something amazing.</p>
    </div>
  )
}

// Arrow function syntax — same result
const WelcomeMessage = () => (
  <div>
    <h2>Welcome to React!</h2>
  </div>
)
```

---

## Rendering a Component Multiple Times

```jsx
// Write once, use anywhere!
function Greeting() {
  return <h1>Hello! 👋</h1>
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Greeting />
      <Greeting />
      <Greeting />
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Props — Passing Data to Components

Props let you pass data from a parent to a child, just like HTML attributes.

```jsx
// Passing and receiving props
function UserCard(props) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{props.name}</h2>
      <p>Age: {props.age}</p>
      <p>Role: {props.role}</p>
    </div>
  )
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <UserCard name="Alice" age={28} role="Developer" />
      <UserCard name="Bob" age={35} role="Designer" />
      <UserCard name="Charlie" age={22} role="Student" />
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Destructuring Props

```jsx
// Cleaner: destructure props directly in the signature
function UserCard({ name, age, role }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{name}</h2>
      <p>Age: {age}</p>
      <p>Role: {role}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Default Props

```jsx
// Set defaults so props are optional
function Button({ label = "Click Me", color = "blue", size = "medium" }) {
  return (
    <button
      style={{
        backgroundColor: color,
        padding: size === "large" ? "12px 24px" : "8px 16px",
        color: 'white',
        border: 'none',
        borderRadius: '4px',
        cursor: 'pointer',
      }}
    >
      {label}
    </button>
  )
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Button />
      <Button label="Submit" color="green" />
      <Button label="Delete" color="red" size="large" />
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## The children Prop

```jsx
// children renders whatever is between the component tags
function Card({ title, children }) {
  return (
    <div style={{ border: '1px solid #ddd', borderRadius: '8px', padding: '20px', margin: '10px' }}>
      <h3>{title}</h3>
      <hr />
      {children}
    </div>
  )
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Card title="User Info">
        <p>Name: Alice</p>
        <p>Email: alice@example.com</p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Product">
        <p>Price: $29.99</p>
        <button>Add to Cart</button>
      </Card>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Props Are Read-Only

```jsx
// ❌ WRONG — never mutate props
function Bad(props) {
  props.name = "changed"  // ERROR!
  return <h1>{props.name}</h1>
}

// ✅ CORRECT — only read props
function Good({ name }) {
  return <h1>{name}</h1>
}
```

---

## Building a Component Tree

```jsx
// Assemble small components into a full layout
function Logo() {
  return <span style={{ fontWeight: 'bold', fontSize: '24px' }}>MyApp</span>
}

function NavLinks() {
  return (
    <nav>
      <a href="/">Home</a> | <a href="/about">About</a>
    </nav>
  )
}

function Navbar() {
  return (
    <header style={{ display: 'flex', justifyContent: 'space-between', padding: '16px', background: '#1e293b', color: 'white' }}>
      <Logo />
      <NavLinks />
    </header>
  )
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Navbar />
      <main style={{ padding: '20px' }}>
        <h1>Welcome!</h1>
      </main>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Chapter Summary

- Components are reusable UI pieces — functions that return JSX
- Names must start with a capital letter
- Props pass data parent → child (one-directional, read-only)
- Use destructuring for cleaner prop access
- The children prop renders nested JSX content

## What Is State?

**State** is data that lives inside a component and can change over time. When state changes, React automatically re-renders the component.

- Props = data from OUTSIDE (read-only)
- State = data managed INSIDE (can change)

Real examples: counter value, modal open/closed, text in an input, shopping cart items.

---

## The Problem Without State

```jsx
// ❌ Regular variables don't cause re-renders
function Counter() {
  let count = 0

  function handleClick() {
    count = count + 1
    // Screen never updates — React doesn't know count changed!
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={handleClick}>Add 1</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## useState — The Solution

```jsx
// useState triggers a re-render when the value changes
import { useState } from 'react'

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
  //      ↑         ↑              ↑
  //   current   update fn     starting value

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Count: {count}</h2>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>+ Add 1</button>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count - 1)}>- Subtract 1</button>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(0)}>Reset</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Multiple State Variables

```jsx
// Call useState multiple times for independent state values
import { useState } from 'react'

function UserProfile() {
  const [name, setName] = useState("Alice")
  const [age, setAge] = useState(25)
  const [isOnline, setIsOnline] = useState(false)

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{name} — {isOnline ? "🟢 Online" : "🔴 Offline"}</h2>
      <p>Age: {age}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setAge(age + 1)}>Birthday! 🎂</button>
      <button onClick={() => setIsOnline(!isOnline)}>Toggle Status</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## State with Objects

```jsx
// Always spread old state when updating objects!
import { useState } from 'react'

function UserForm() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: '', email: '' })

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        value={user.name}
        onChange={(e) => setUser({ ...user, name: e.target.value })}
        placeholder="Name"
      />
      <input
        value={user.email}
        onChange={(e) => setUser({ ...user, email: e.target.value })}
        placeholder="Email"
      />
      <p>Name: {user.name} | Email: {user.email}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## State with Arrays

```jsx
// Add and remove items from an array in state
import { useState } from 'react'

function TodoList() {
  const [todos, setTodos] = useState(['Buy groceries', 'Read React docs'])
  const [newTodo, setNewTodo] = useState('')

  function addTodo() {
    if (!newTodo.trim()) return
    setTodos([...todos, newTodo])
    setNewTodo('')
  }

  function removeTodo(index) {
    setTodos(todos.filter((_, i) => i !== index))
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <input value={newTodo} onChange={(e) => setNewTodo(e.target.value)} placeholder="New todo..." />
      <button onClick={addTodo}>Add</button>
      <ul>
        {todos.map((todo, i) => (
          <li key={i}>
            {todo} <button onClick={() => removeTodo(i)}>❌</button>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Functional State Updates

```jsx
// Use prev => prev + 1 when new state depends on old state
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

  function addThree() {
    // ❌ Wrong — all 3 calls see the same stale count
    // setCount(count + 1)  // 0+1=1
    // setCount(count + 1)  // 0+1=1 (still!)
    // setCount(count + 1)  // 0+1=1 (still!)

    // ✅ Correct — each call gets the latest prev value
    setCount(prev => prev + 1)
    setCount(prev => prev + 1)
    setCount(prev => prev + 1)
    // Result: count goes to 3
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Count: {count}</h2>
      <button onClick={addThree}>Add 3</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Common Mistakes

```jsx
// ❌ Never mutate state directly
const [items, setItems] = useState([1, 2, 3])
items.push(4)          // WRONG
setItems(items)        // React won't re-render!

// ✅ Always create a new array/object
setItems([...items, 4])

// ❌ Never mutate state objects
const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Alice' })
user.name = 'Bob'      // WRONG
setUser(user)          // No re-render!

// ✅ Correct
setUser({ ...user, name: 'Bob' })
```

---

## Chapter Summary

- State is internal data that, when changed, causes a re-render
- useState(initialValue) returns [value, setter]
- Never mutate state — always call the setter with a new value
- For objects/arrays, spread the old state and override what changed
- Use functional updates when state depends on its previous value

## React Events vs HTML Events

```jsx
// HTML uses strings and lowercase
<button onclick="handleClick()">Click</button>

// JSX uses functions and camelCase
<button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>
```

Key differences:
- Event names are **camelCase** (onClick, not onclick)
- Pass a **function reference**, not a string
- React wraps native events in **SyntheticEvent** for cross-browser support

---

## Click Events

```jsx
// Different ways to handle click
import { useState } from 'react'

function ClickDemo() {
  const [message, setMessage] = useState("Nothing clicked yet")

  function handleClick() {
    setMessage("Button was clicked! 🎉")
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <p>{message}</p>
      <button onClick={handleClick}>Named Handler</button>
      <button onClick={() => setMessage("Inline clicked!")}>Inline Handler</button>

      {/* ❌ WRONG — this runs handleClick immediately on render */}
      {/* <button onClick={handleClick()}>Wrong!</button> */}
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## The Event Object

```jsx
// React passes a SyntheticEvent as the first argument
function EventInfo() {
  function handleClick(event) {
    console.log(event.type)       // "click"
    console.log(event.target)     // The clicked element
    console.log(event.target.id)  // Element ID
    console.log(event.clientX)    // Mouse X position
    event.preventDefault()        // Stop default behavior
  }

  return <button id="info-btn" onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>
}
```

---

## Input Change Event

```jsx
// Controlled input — React fully controls the input value
import { useState } from 'react'

function LiveInput() {
  const [text, setText] = useState('')

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="text"
        value={text}
        onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Type something..."
      />
      <p>You typed: <strong>{text}</strong></p>
      <p>Characters: {text.length}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Keyboard Events

```jsx
// Send on Enter, clear on Escape
import { useState } from 'react'

function ChatInput() {
  const [messages, setMessages] = useState([])
  const [input, setInput] = useState('')

  function handleKeyDown(event) {
    if (event.key === 'Enter' && input.trim()) {
      setMessages([...messages, input])
      setInput('')
    }
    if (event.key === 'Escape') {
      setInput('')
    }
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        value={input}
        onChange={(e) => setInput(e.target.value)}
        onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
        placeholder="Type and press Enter..."
      />
      <ul>
        {messages.map((msg, i) => <li key={i}>{msg}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Form Submit Event

```jsx
// Always call event.preventDefault() in form handlers
import { useState } from 'react'

function LoginForm() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('')
  const [password, setPassword] = useState('')
  const [submitted, setSubmitted] = useState(false)

  function handleSubmit(event) {
    event.preventDefault()   // Prevent page reload!

    if (!email || !password) {
      alert('Fill all fields')
      return
    }

    setSubmitted(true)
  }

  if (submitted) return <h2>✅ Logged in as {email}!</h2>

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input type="email" value={email} onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)} placeholder="Email" required />
      <input type="password" value={password} onChange={(e) => setPassword(e.target.value)} placeholder="Password" required />
      <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>
  )
}
```

---

## Passing Arguments to Handlers

```jsx
// Use arrow functions to pass arguments
function ProductList() {
  const products = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Laptop', price: 999 },
    { id: 2, name: 'Phone', price: 699 },
  ]

  function handleBuy(id, name) {
    alert('Buying: ' + name + ' (ID: ' + id + ')')
  }

  return (
    <ul>
      {products.map(product => (
        <li key={product.id}>
          {product.name} — ${product.price}
          <button onClick={() => handleBuy(product.id, product.name)}>
            Buy Now
          </button>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

---

## Chapter Summary

- React events use camelCase (onClick, onChange, onSubmit)
- Pass a function reference, never call it during render
- The event object is the first parameter to any handler
- Always call event.preventDefault() in form submit handlers
- Use arrow functions to pass extra arguments to handlers

## Rendering Lists with .map()

In React, use JavaScript's .map() to transform an array into JSX elements:

```jsx
// Simple list rendering with .map()
function FruitList() {
  const fruits = ['Apple', 'Banana', 'Grapes', 'Orange']

  return (
    <ul>
      {fruits.map((fruit, index) => (
        <li key={index}>{fruit}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

---

## The key Prop — Critical!

React needs a key prop to track which items changed, added, or removed:

```jsx
// Always use unique IDs as keys — avoid indexes for dynamic lists
const users = [
  { id: 'u1', name: 'Alice' },
  { id: 'u2', name: 'Bob' },
]

// ✅ Best practice — use ID
users.map(user => <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>)

// ⚠️ Acceptable for static lists
users.map((user, index) => <li key={index}>{user.name}</li>)
```

Without keys, React re-renders ALL list items on any change. With unique keys, React updates only what changed.

---

## Rendering a List of Components

```jsx
// Mapping data to reusable components
function ProductCard({ name, price }) {
  return (
    <div style={{ border: '1px solid #ccc', padding: '16px', borderRadius: '8px', margin: '8px' }}>
      <h3>{name}</h3>
      <p>${price}</p>
    </div>
  )
}

function ProductGrid() {
  const products = [
    { id: 1, name: 'MacBook Pro', price: 1999 },
    { id: 2, name: 'iPhone 15', price: 999 },
    { id: 3, name: 'iPad Air', price: 749 },
  ]

  return (
    <div style={{ display: 'grid', gridTemplateColumns: 'repeat(3, 1fr)', gap: '16px' }}>
      {products.map(product => (
        <ProductCard key={product.id} name={product.name} price={product.price} />
      ))}
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Conditional Rendering: if/else

```jsx
// Return different JSX based on a condition
function StatusMessage({ status }) {
  if (status === 'loading') return <div>Loading...</div>
  if (status === 'error') return <div>Something went wrong!</div>
  return <div>Data loaded successfully!</div>
}
```

---

## Conditional Rendering: Ternary

```jsx
// Ternary for toggling between two options inline
function UserStatus({ isLoggedIn }) {
  return (
    <div>
      {isLoggedIn
        ? <p>Welcome back!</p>
        : <p>Please log in.</p>
      }
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Conditional Rendering: && Operator

```jsx
// && renders only when condition is true, nothing otherwise
function Dashboard({ hasAlert, alertMessage }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      {hasAlert && (
        <div style={{ background: '#fef3c7', padding: '12px', borderRadius: '4px' }}>
          {alertMessage}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Full Example: Task Manager

```jsx
// Lists + conditionals + state combined
import { useState } from 'react'

function TaskManager() {
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState([
    { id: 1, text: 'Learn React', done: true },
    { id: 2, text: 'Build a project', done: false },
    { id: 3, text: 'Get a job', done: false },
  ])

  function toggleTask(id) {
    setTasks(tasks.map(task =>
      task.id === id ? { ...task, done: !task.done } : task
    ))
  }

  const completedCount = tasks.filter(t => t.done).length

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Tasks ({completedCount}/{tasks.length} done)</h2>
      {tasks.length === 0 ? (
        <p>All done!</p>
      ) : (
        <ul>
          {tasks.map(task => (
            <li
              key={task.id}
              onClick={() => toggleTask(task.id)}
              style={{
                textDecoration: task.done ? 'line-through' : 'none',
                cursor: 'pointer',
                padding: '8px',
                listStyle: 'none',
              }}
            >
              {task.done ? 'Done:' : 'Todo:'} {task.text}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Chapter Summary

- Use .map() to transform arrays into JSX
- Always provide a unique key prop — prefer IDs over indexes
- if/else: best for returning completely different components
- Ternary: best for toggling between two JSX blocks inline
- &&: best for "show or show nothing" conditions

## What Are Side Effects?

A side effect is anything that happens outside of rendering UI:
- Fetching data from an API
- Setting up timers (setTimeout, setInterval)
- Updating the document title
- Adding/removing event listeners

React's render function should be pure — same inputs, same output, no side effects. useEffect is where you put all side effects.

---

## useEffect Syntax

```jsx
// Basic useEffect structure
import { useEffect } from 'react'

useEffect(
  () => {
    // Side effect code — runs AFTER render

    return () => {
      // Optional cleanup
      // Runs before component unmounts or before effect re-runs
    }
  },
  [/* dependency array */]
)
```

---

## The Three Dependency Array Patterns

```jsx
// Pattern 1: No array — runs after EVERY render
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Runs after every render")
})

// Pattern 2: Empty [] — runs ONCE on mount
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Runs once when component appears")
}, [])

// Pattern 3: With dependencies — runs when those change
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Runs when count or name changes")
}, [count, name])
```

---

## Example 1: Update Document Title

```jsx
// Sync the browser tab title with state
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react'

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = 'Count: ' + count
  }, [count])

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Count: {count}</h2>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Example 2: Fetch Data on Mount

```jsx
// Fetch users once when component loads
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react'

function UsersList() {
  const [users, setUsers] = useState([])
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true)
  const [error, setError] = useState(null)

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users')
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => {
        setUsers(data)
        setLoading(false)
      })
      .catch(err => {
        setError(err.message)
        setLoading(false)
      })
  }, [])

  if (loading) return <div>Loading users...</div>
  if (error) return <div>Error: {error}</div>

  return (
    <ul>
      {users.map(user => (
        <li key={user.id}>
          <strong>{user.name}</strong> — {user.email}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

---

## Example 3: Timer with Cleanup

```jsx
// Stopwatch — clears interval on unmount to prevent memory leaks
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react'

function Stopwatch() {
  const [seconds, setSeconds] = useState(0)
  const [running, setRunning] = useState(false)

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!running) return

    const intervalId = setInterval(() => {
      setSeconds(prev => prev + 1)
    }, 1000)

    // Always return cleanup to prevent memory leaks!
    return () => clearInterval(intervalId)
  }, [running])

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{seconds}s</h2>
      <button onClick={() => setRunning(!running)}>
        {running ? 'Pause' : 'Start'}
      </button>
      <button onClick={() => { setRunning(false); setSeconds(0) }}>Reset</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Example 4: Window Resize Listener

```jsx
// Track window width — remove listener on unmount
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react'

function WindowSize() {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth)

  useEffect(() => {
    function handleResize() {
      setWidth(window.innerWidth)
    }

    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize)

    // Cleanup: remove the listener when component unmounts
    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize)
  }, [])

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Width: {width}px</p>
      <p>{width < 768 ? 'Mobile' : 'Desktop'}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

