## Common Mistakes

```jsx
// ❌ Missing dependency causes stale data bug
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = 'Count: ' + count
}, [])
// count changes but the title never updates!

// ✅ Fix: Add count to dependencies
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = 'Count: ' + count
}, [count])

// ❌ No cleanup causes memory leak
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(() => console.log("tick"), 1000)
  // Timer keeps running even after component is gone!
}, [])

// ✅ Fix: Always return cleanup
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(() => console.log("tick"), 1000)
  return () => clearInterval(timer)
}, [])
```

---

## Chapter Summary

- useEffect is for side effects: API calls, timers, event listeners
- No dependency array: runs after every render
- Empty []: runs once on mount
- [deps]: runs when listed values change
- Always return a cleanup function to prevent memory leaks

## The Challenge: Sibling Communication

React data flows **one direction** — from parent to child via props. But what if two sibling components need to share the same data?

The answer is **Lifting State Up** — move the shared state to the closest common parent, then pass it down as props.

---

## Before Lifting State — The Problem

```jsx
// ❌ Two components with their own isolated state — they can't talk to each other
function Celsius() {
  const [value, setValue] = useState('')
  return <input value={value} onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)} placeholder="Celsius" />
}

function Fahrenheit() {
  const [value, setValue] = useState('')
  return <input value={value} onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)} placeholder="Fahrenheit" />
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Celsius />
      <Fahrenheit />
      {/* These two have NO connection to each other */}
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## After Lifting State — The Solution

```jsx
// ✅ State lives in the parent — both children receive it as props
import { useState } from 'react'

// Child components receive value and onChange as props
function CelsiusInput({ celsius, onChange }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <label>Celsius:</label>
      <input
        type="number"
        value={celsius}
        onChange={e => onChange(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Enter celsius"
      />
    </div>
  )
}

function FahrenheitInput({ celsius, onChange }) {
  // Convert celsius to fahrenheit for display
  const fahrenheit = celsius !== '' ? (celsius * 9/5 + 32).toFixed(1) : ''

  function handleChange(e) {
    // Convert back to celsius when fahrenheit changes
    const f = e.target.value
    const c = f !== '' ? ((f - 32) * 5/9).toFixed(1) : ''
    onChange(c)
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <label>Fahrenheit:</label>
      <input
        type="number"
        value={fahrenheit}
        onChange={handleChange}
        placeholder="Enter fahrenheit"
      />
    </div>
  )
}

// Parent owns the state
function TemperatureConverter() {
  const [celsius, setCelsius] = useState('')

  return (
    <div style={{ padding: '20px', maxWidth: '300px' }}>
      <h2>Temperature Converter</h2>
      <CelsiusInput celsius={celsius} onChange={setCelsius} />
      <FahrenheitInput celsius={celsius} onChange={setCelsius} />
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Lifting State — The Pattern

```text
Before Lifting:              After Lifting:
  Parent                       Parent (owns state)
  ├── Child A (state A)         ├── Child A (gets state as prop)
  └── Child B (state B)         └── Child B (gets state as prop)

  A and B can't share           A and B both read/write
  state with each other         the same state via parent
```

---

## Prop Drilling — When Lifting Becomes a Problem

When you lift state many levels up, you may need to pass props through components that don't use them:

```jsx
// ❌ Prop drilling — middle components pass props they don't need
function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Alice', role: 'admin' })
  return <Dashboard user={user} />   // Dashboard doesn't need user...
}

function Dashboard({ user }) {
  return <Sidebar user={user} />     // Sidebar doesn't need user...
}

function Sidebar({ user }) {
  return <UserAvatar user={user} />  // Only this component needs user
}

function UserAvatar({ user }) {
  return <img src={user.avatar} alt={user.name} />  // Finally used here!
}
```

This becomes painful with deeply nested components. The solution is **Context API** (Chapter 10).

---

## Callback Props — Child Communicates Back to Parent

```jsx
// Parent passes a callback, child calls it with data
import { useState } from 'react'

function SearchBar({ onSearch }) {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('')

  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault()
    onSearch(query)   // ← Call the parent's function with data
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input
        value={query}
        onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Search..."
      />
      <button type="submit">Search</button>
    </form>
  )
}

function SearchResults({ results }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {results.map((r, i) => <li key={i}>{r}</li>)}
    </ul>
  )
}

function App() {
  const [results, setResults] = useState([])
  const allData = ['React', 'JavaScript', 'Node.js', 'CSS', 'HTML']

  function handleSearch(query) {
    const filtered = allData.filter(item =>
      item.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase())
    )
    setResults(filtered)
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <SearchBar onSearch={handleSearch} />
      <SearchResults results={results} />
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## Chapter Summary

- Data flows **down** (parent to child via props), events flow **up** (child to parent via callbacks)
- **Lift state** to the closest common ancestor when siblings need to share it
- Pass a **callback prop** for children to communicate back to the parent
- **Prop drilling** becomes painful at 3+ levels deep — use Context API (Chapter 10) instead

## What Is React Router?

React Router is the standard library for **client-side navigation** in React apps. Instead of the browser loading a new HTML page on each click, React Router **swaps out components** — making navigation feel instant.

```bash
// Install React Router
npm install react-router-dom
```

