## When NOT to Use Context

Context is NOT a replacement for all state. Use it only for **truly global** data:

| Use Context For | Don't Use Context For |
|----------------|----------------------|
| Theme / dark mode | Local UI state (modal open) |
| Logged-in user | Form field values |
| Language / locale | Component-specific state |
| Shopping cart | Data that only one component uses |

---

## Chapter Summary

- Context eliminates **prop drilling** for truly global data
- Three steps: `createContext` → `Provider` (wraps app) → `useContext` (reads value)
- Wrap context logic in a **custom hook** for cleaner consumer code
- Common use cases: theme, auth, locale, cart
- Don't overuse Context — local state is fine for component-specific data

## useRef — Access DOM Elements and Persist Values

`useRef` gives you a **mutable container** that persists across renders without causing re-renders when it changes.

Two main uses:
1. Access a DOM element directly
2. Store a value that shouldn't trigger re-renders

```jsx
// Use 1: Access a DOM element
import { useRef, useEffect } from 'react'

function AutoFocusInput() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null)   // Create a ref

  useEffect(() => {
    inputRef.current.focus()       // Access the real DOM element
  }, [])

  return <input ref={inputRef} placeholder="I auto-focus on mount!" />
}
```

```jsx
// Use 2: Store a value that shouldn't cause re-renders
import { useRef, useState, useEffect } from 'react'

function Stopwatch() {
  const [time, setTime] = useState(0)
  const [running, setRunning] = useState(false)
  const intervalRef = useRef(null)   // Store the interval ID

  function start() {
    setRunning(true)
    intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => {
      setTime(t => t + 1)
    }, 1000)
    // intervalRef.current does NOT cause a re-render when changed
  }

  function stop() {
    setRunning(false)
    clearInterval(intervalRef.current)
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Time: {time}s</p>
      <button onClick={start} disabled={running}>Start</button>
      <button onClick={stop} disabled={!running}>Stop</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## useReducer — Complex State Logic

`useReducer` is an alternative to `useState` for managing complex state with multiple related values and specific update logic.

```jsx
// useReducer pattern — great for state machines
import { useReducer } from 'react'

// 1. Define the reducer (pure function)
function cartReducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'ADD_ITEM':
      return {
        ...state,
        items: [...state.items, action.payload],
        total: state.total + action.payload.price,
      }
    case 'REMOVE_ITEM':
      const removed = state.items.find(i => i.id === action.payload)
      return {
        ...state,
        items: state.items.filter(i => i.id !== action.payload),
        total: state.total - (removed?.price || 0),
      }
    case 'CLEAR_CART':
      return { items: [], total: 0 }
    default:
      return state
  }
}

// 2. Use the reducer in a component
function ShoppingCart() {
  const [cart, dispatch] = useReducer(cartReducer, { items: [], total: 0 })

  function addItem(product) {
    dispatch({ type: 'ADD_ITEM', payload: product })
  }

  function removeItem(id) {
    dispatch({ type: 'REMOVE_ITEM', payload: id })
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Cart ({cart.items.length} items) — Total: ${cart.total}</h2>
      {cart.items.map(item => (
        <div key={item.id}>
          {item.name} — ${item.price}
          <button onClick={() => removeItem(item.id)}>Remove</button>
        </div>
      ))}
      <button onClick={() => addItem({ id: Date.now(), name: 'Widget', price: 9.99 })}>
        Add Widget
      </button>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'CLEAR_CART' })}>Clear Cart</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## useMemo — Cache Expensive Calculations

`useMemo` memoizes the **result** of an expensive function so it only recalculates when its dependencies change.

```jsx
// Without useMemo — expensiveCalc runs on EVERY render
function ProductList({ products, filter }) {
  // This filters every time the component re-renders, even for unrelated changes
  const filtered = products.filter(p => p.category === filter)

  return <ul>{filtered.map(p => <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>)}</ul>
}
```

```jsx
// With useMemo — only recalculates when products or filter changes
import { useMemo } from 'react'

function ProductList({ products, filter }) {
  const filtered = useMemo(() => {
    console.log('Filtering products...')  // Only logs when products or filter changes
    return products.filter(p => p.category === filter)
  }, [products, filter])   // ← Dependencies

  return <ul>{filtered.map(p => <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>)}</ul>
}
```

---

## useCallback — Cache Event Handler Functions

`useCallback` memoizes a **function** so it's not recreated on every render. Useful when passing callbacks to optimized child components.

```jsx
// Without useCallback — handleClick is a new function on every render
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

  const handleClick = () => {   // New function every render!
    console.log('clicked')
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <HeavyChild onClick={handleClick} />
    </div>
  )
}
```

```jsx
// With useCallback — handleClick is stable between renders
import { useCallback } from 'react'

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

  // handleClick is the SAME function reference between renders
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log('clicked')
  }, [])  // ← Empty array = created once, never recreated

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <HeavyChild onClick={handleClick} />  {/* Won't re-render unnecessarily */}
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

## When to Use Each Hook

```text
useRef         → Access a DOM element, OR store a value without re-rendering
               (interval IDs, previous values, timers)

useReducer     → Complex state with multiple actions and related fields
               (shopping cart, form wizard, state machines)

useMemo        → Cache an EXPENSIVE COMPUTED VALUE
               (filtering large lists, sorting, calculations)

useCallback    → Cache a FUNCTION REFERENCE
               (event handlers passed to memoized child components)
```

---

## Chapter Summary

- `useRef` accesses DOM elements and stores mutable values without re-renders
- `useReducer` manages complex state with a predictable reducer function
- `useMemo` caches expensive computed values — only recalculates on dependency changes
- `useCallback` caches function references — prevents unnecessary child re-renders
- Don't over-optimize: only add useMemo/useCallback when you have measured a performance issue

## The Three Data States

Every data-fetching UI has three states you must handle:

```text
Loading  → Show a spinner or skeleton
Success  → Show the data
Error    → Show an error message with retry option
```

Always handle all three. Never skip the error state.

